WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.280
A double neutron star system with the lowest total mass recorded so far has been identified by radio observations. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 211. Astronomers identified the lowest mass double neutron star system observed to date through radio pulsar timing. Neutron stars in the system are the dense remnants of massive stars that ended in supernovae. Some neutron stars appear as pulsars when their beams sweep past Earth.

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:33.920
The discovery adds a new data point for models of binary stellar evolution and supernova

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:34.680
mass loss.

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:41.320
Timing measurements allow precise mass determinations that refine understanding of core collapse outcomes.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:45.760
The system adds a critical calibration point for supernova models.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:51.340
Radio timing measurements reveal orbital parameters that constrain the mass lost during each

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:52.120
star's collapse.

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:57.880
This system sits below previously known examples and tightens the allowed range for remnant

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:04.440
masses after iron core collapse. Imagine a pair of stars each eight times the sun's mass, orbiting

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:10.380
only a few astronomical units apart. When the first star exhausts its fuel and explodes,

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:15.840
the surviving companion must remain bound, despite losing several solar masses in seconds.

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:21.600
The second star later follows the same path, leaving two ultra-dense remnants whose combined

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.000
find mass is less than three solar masses.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:29.420
Radio telescopes detect the pulsars' regular pulses and measure tiny shifts in arrival

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:31.540
times caused by the companion's gravity.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.680
Those timing residuals yield masses accurate to a few percent.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:41.280
The puzzle is why so little mass remains, since standard models predict more material

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:44.500
ejected, yet this system shows the opposite outcome.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:50.420
Next, James Webb Space Telescope data indicate the solar system may have experienced more

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:55.440
than one phase of extreme debris disks, similar to the arrow when Earth's silica-rich crust

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:01.060
formed. Outward migration of gas giants could have scattered smaller bodies and generated

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:06.480
dust-rich conditions at different times. The observations link disk brightness variations

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:12.920
to dynamical events in young planetary systems. Observations show silica-rich debris disks form

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:19.100
from collisions between Mars-sized bodies around younger stars. Silica-poor disks appear linked to

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:24.660
smaller scale collisions across a wider range of stellar ages and show more variation in brightness.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:28.760
Web verified that extreme debris discs vary in brightness,

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:34.620
contain smaller dust grains and hold high concentrations of warm dust close to their stars.

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:38.920
These discs were first identified by spitzer and remain rare.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:44.020
The new measurements refined grain size distributions and temperature profiles in these

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:50.200
active systems. NASA chose the Prima probe explorer as its next astrophysics mission in the probe

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:56.780
class. The mission will conduct far infrared observations to study star formation, galaxy evolution,

00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:02.480
and the build-up of heavy elements. Prima carries a cryogenic telescope and sensitive detectors

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:07.860
optimized for wavelengths blocked by Earth's atmosphere. It represents the first mission of its

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:13.680
kind in the probe explorer line. The telescope designed targets regions where dust obscures visible

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:14.780
light observations.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:21.340
ESA's Jupiter-IC moons explorer recorded a series of images during its September 28th

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:22.760
Earth gravity assist flyby.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:28.200
The monitoring cameras showed Earth as a thin crescent than a full disc with Africa and

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:28.940
the moon visible.

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:35.280
The flyby increased the spacecraft's speed by 3.5 kilometers per second and altered its

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:36.760
trajectory by 20 degrees.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:42.040
The images provide public views of the maneuver captured by onboard monitoring systems.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:48.800
ESA astronaut Sophie Adinot performed three extra vehicular activities totaling 19 hours

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:54.260
and 42 minutes outside the International Space Station. The spacewalks included hardware

00:03:54.260 --> 00:04:00.480
replacement, installation of a new communications antenna, maintenance tasks, and experiment preparation.

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:07.000
Adinot served as EV1 on her third outing after adapting to the challenges of working in vacuum

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:12.560
and extreme temperatures. The sequence demonstrated rapid recovery and increasing responsibility

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:19.940
across consecutive outings. On October 2, 480 before the common era, a solar eclipse darkened

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:25.620
the sky during a standoff near Corinth between Spartan forces under Cleombrotus and Persian

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:32.020
troops led by Xerxes I. Herodotus recorded that Cleombrotus interpreted the sudden darkening

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:35.180
as an unfavorable omen while preparing to sacrifice.

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:39.140
The Spartan commander ordered a full retreat rather than engage.

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:44.820
Modern astronomers have dated the event to that specific day based on historical and orbital

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:45.480
calculations.

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:50.800
The account provides one of the earliest written records linking celestial events to military

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:51.640
decisions.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:57.680
Grace Potter released the track's Spacewalking Woman, co-written with NASA astronaut Jessica

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:02.760
of MIR. The song draws on MIR's experience doing space walks on the International Space

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:08.080
Station. MIR contributed lyrics reflecting the view of Earth from orbit and the physical

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:13.280
demands of working outside the station. The collaboration pairs Potter's musical style

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:18.380
with firsthand accounts from orbital operations. The release highlights connections between

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:24.480
spaceflight experiences and creative expression. AstroPort Space Technologies secured a NASA

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:30.180
a small business innovation research phase 2E award for lunar surface infrastructure work.

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:35.720
The award requires matching funds, which Knight Aerospace provided through an equity investment.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:42.300
The partnership focuses on modular pallet systems, landing pads, hall roads, and dust-resistant

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:46.280
containment hardware designed for lunar gravity and temperature extremes.

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:52.420
Knight brings certified manufacturing processes and roll-on roll-off cargo expertise to the

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:52.680
effort.

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:58.800
The collaboration aims to advance autonomous construction capabilities for future lunar operations.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:05.060
Norwegian authorities conducted jammer tests on Andoya Island, launching hundreds of jamming and

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:10.840
spoofing attacks against positioning signals. Participants tested receivers and algorithms under

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:17.000
realistic interference conditions at 69 degrees north. The week-long campaign helps develop more

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:21.620
More resilient navigation solutions for aviation, maritime, and space applications.

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:26.040
The Arctic location provides challenging signal environments for testing.

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:32.780
A false color polarized radar image from Copernicus Sentinel-1 shows sea surface roughness beneath

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:34.060
typhoon surrogye.

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:39.000
The radar instrument penetrates cloud cover to map wind-driven wave patterns across

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:39.580
the ocean.

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:44.780
The image reveals the storm's footprint on the water, even where visible light observations

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:45.560
are obscured.

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:50.920
Polarization data enhanced contrast in surface features created by high winds.

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:58.720
Today's astronomy picture of the day displays the entire sharpness catalog containing 313 H2 regions.

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:05.020
The composite image allows viewers to zoom across the full set of emission nebulae cataloged by Stuart

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:11.500
Sharpless. Each object represents a site of active star formation ionized by young massive stars.

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:17.100
The catalog serves as a comprehensive reference for studying ionized gas structures in the Milky

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:17.460
Way.

